work Communication\",\n            \"explanation\": \"A core function of a GATEWAY is to handle 'PROTOCOLOS DISTINTOS' (distinct protocols), allowing systems with incompatible communication methods to exchange data. This capability is vital for enabling 'REDE HETEROGÊNEA' (heterogeneous networks) to communicate effectively [Data: Entities (640), Relationships (780, 792)]. Gateways act as translators or intermediaries, converting information from one protocol into a format understandable by another, which is crucial for integrating diverse technological environments and supporting complex, interconnected infrastructures [Data: Relationships (780)]. This ability makes GATEWAYs indispensable in scenarios requiring interoperability between systems with different communication languages [Data: Entities (640), Relationships (780)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Network Address Translation (NAT) for IP Address Conversion\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Network Address Translation (NAT) is a key process often performed by GATEWAYs, which remaps one IP address space into another by modifying the network address information in data packets [Data: Entities (632), Relationships (782)]. This functionality, also referred to as address conversion, allows devices with private IP addresses on a local network to communicate with devices on the internet using a single public IP address [Data: Entities (632), Relationships (782)]. NAT is frequently implemented by gateways to translate internal IP addresses to public ones, facilitating efficient use of public IP addresses and providing a layer of security by obscuring the internal network structure from external networks [Data: Entities (632), Relationships (782)]. The NAT entity has a degree of 2, indicating its direct association with gateway functionalities [Data: Entities (632)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Gateway's role in Internet connectivity and Corporate Networks\",\n         